{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "pon-01-turn-number-statements-into-equations",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Turning Number Statements into Equations",
    "description": "Turn one-number statements into equations, undo operations in the right order, and keep only answers that fit the question.",
    "teachingApproach": "Open with an equal-distance puzzle so the learner sees that a sentence can become an equation. Then move from linear statements to fractional parts, multi-step operation stories, simple quadratics, root selection and a mixed-fraction capstone. Every new form is demonstrated with actual numbers before a fresh parallel question, and each answer is checked against the original statement.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 30,
    "sourceScope": "Teach the chapter's core habit: give the unknown number a symbol, translate the verbal condition exactly, solve the equation, and check the result in the original sentence. Begin with equal-distance language such as 'as much greater than 36 as less than 86', then cover times-a-number statements, fractional parts of one number, multi-step operations, and equations where the number is multiplied by a fraction of itself or combined with its square. Show that quadratic equations can produce two algebraic roots but a positive/natural-number condition may remove one. Include a capstone mixing two fractional parts on opposite sides. Keep two-number systems and digit-place puzzles for later scripts."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's learn how number riddles turn into equations.\n\nA sentence like 'a number is as much above 40 as it is below 90' looks wordy, but it hides one simple balance. Once that balance is visible, the puzzle becomes ordinary arithmetic.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A number puzzle becomes much easier when the sentence turns into one clean equation.\n\nStart with this: a number is as much above 40 as it is below 90.\n\nThe two gaps are equal.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let the number be x.\n\nGap above 40: x − 40\nGap below 90: 90 − x\n\nSo:\nx − 40 = 90 − x\n2x = 130\nx = 65\n\n65 sits exactly halfway between 40 and 90.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
